worst_disagreements.annotated_submission:
Hier der eingereichte Text mit farblich gekennzeichneten Kommentaren:

Wir haben  
φ(N) = (p−1)(q−1) = N − p − q + 1,  
also φ(N) ≈ N  
\textcolor{yellow}{(Hier wäre kurz zu erwähnen, dass der relative Fehler O(1/√N) ist und damit vernachlässigbar klein.)}  
mit einem Fehler in der Größenordnung √N (da p und q etwa gleichgroß sind).  
\textcolor{green}{(Richtig: φ(N)=N−(p+q)+1 und |p+q−1|=O(√N) bei p,q≈√N.)}

Außerdem ist  
ed = 1 + k·φ(N) für ein k ∈ ℕ mit k < e.  
\textcolor{green}{(Korrekt: ed≡1 mod φ(N) und daher k=(ed−1)/φ(N)<e.)}

Daraus folgt  
d = (1 + k·φ(N)) / e für dieses k.  
\textcolor{green}{(Formel stimmt.)}

Die führenden Bits von d können nun über die bereits erwähnte Approximation φ(N) ≈ N ermittelt werden,  
da damit d ≈ (1 + kN)/e ist.  
\textcolor{yellow}{(Man sollte abschätzen, wie groß der Unterschied Δ = k·(N−φ(N))/e ≈ k·(p+q)/e ist, um zu sehen, wie viele oberste Bits stabil sind.)}

Hierbei gibt es noch ≈2^16 Möglichkeiten für k. Eve kann sämtliche Möglichkeiten durchprobieren  
und dasjenige k finden, das mit dem gegebenen Hash-Wert übereinstimmt.  
\textcolor{green}{(Gut: nur etwa 2^16 Kandidaten, Hash-Abgleich wählt das richtige k.)}

Auf diese Weise lernt sie etwa die ersten 1024 Bits von d.  
\textcolor{red}{(Präziser: Die Approximation liefert rund 1023 korrekte oberste Bits. Davon sind 1000 Bits durch den Hash wirklich bestätigt, ~23 Bits darüber hinaus ergeben sich aus der Fehlerabschätzung. Bitte genau angeben, wie viele Bits wirklich sicher sind.)}

Here is your submission with brief inline annotations.  Real mistakes are in red, minor inaccuracies or missing remarks in yellow, and encouraging/helpful notes in green.

---

By Euclid’s theorem on the infinitude of primes, there are infinitely many primes.  \textcolor{green}{Good start—using a classical result.}

By looking at divisibility by two and three, we can see that all primes greater than 3 are either 1 or 5 modulo 6.  \textcolor{green}{Correct classification of residue classes.}

Therefore, there are *either* infinitely many primes of the form $6k+1$ or infinitely many primes of the form $6k+5$.  
\textcolor{yellow}{You should add a short justification: if *both* sets were finite, then there would be only finitely many primes $>3$, contradicting Euclid’s theorem.}

Let $S$ denote an infinite set of such primes in either case.

If the first case holds, then $p + 5$ is divisible by 6 for any $p\in S$.  
\textcolor{green}{Since $p\equiv1\pmod6$, indeed $p+5\equiv0\pmod6$.}  
\textcolor{yellow}{Mention that 5 itself is prime, so $p+5$ is a sum of two primes.}

If the second case holds, then $p + 7$ is divisible by 6 for any $p\in S$.  
\textcolor{green}{Again, $p\equiv5\pmod6$ gives $p+7\equiv0\pmod6$.}  
\textcolor{yellow}{Also note that 7 is prime.}

In either case, we have found infinitely many numbers that are divisible by 6 and that can be written as the sum of two prime numbers.  
\textcolor{green}{Nice conclusion!}

---

Overall this argument is correct once you fill in the few justifications noted above.

Here is your student’s submission with in-line annotations.  Real mistakes are marked in red, minor inaccuracies or clerical points in yellow, and extra pedagogical hints in green.  

---
Since nobody ever dies, and since everyone has at most two children, and since the gender ratio is 1:1, every generation will have exactly 1000 births (since each child has two parents).  
\textcolor{red}{This assumes births per generation stay at 1000; in reality as the living population grows, births per generation grow proportionally, giving exponential rather than linear growth.}

Therefore, initial growth is 1000 births every 30 years, which is a growth rate of $1000/30 \approx 33.33$ births per year.  
\textcolor{red}{Again, this treats the birth rate as constant.  With an ever-increasing population you should write $dN/dt \propto N$, not constant.}  
\textcolor{green}{Often one models $N(t)=N_0\,2^{t/30}$ for “each generation doubles” if every person has two kids.}

After a billion years, the population will be roughly  
$$
1000\times10^9/30 \;\approx\;3.33\times10^{10}
$$  
inhabitants.  
\textcolor{red}{This linear extrapolation is incorrect.  With exponential doubling every 30 years you hit $10^{10}$ far sooner (on the order of centuries, not $10^9$ yr).}  
\textcolor{yellow}{(Dropping the “+1000” initial population here is a negligible numerical slip.)}

This is clearly more than $10^{10}$, so a Malthusian catastrophe will occur.  The first such catastrophe will occur after  
$$
10^{10}/33.33\approx3\times10^8\text{ years}.
$$  
\textcolor{red}{Time to catastrophe should be solved from $2^{t/30}=10^{7}$, giving $t\approx700$ years, not $3\times10^8$ yr.}

It will reduce the population to about $10^9$ people, the vast majority of whom will already have had two children; assuming deaths in the catastrophic state are random, the first Malthusian catastrophe reduces future births to $\approx3.33$ per year, because it reduces the remaining legally breeding population by a factor of 10.  
\textcolor{red}{If the post‐catastrophe population is $10^9$, births per year are $(10^9)/30\approx3.3\times10^7$, not 3.33.  You’ve applied the same linear error here.}

A 700-million year climb follows which takes the final population to  
$$
10^9 + 3.33\cdot7\cdot10^8 \approx2.33\times10^9.
$$  
\textcolor{red}{Again this uses linear growth.  After a catastrophe you’d see exponential regrowth $N(t)=10^9\cdot2^{t/30}$ until the next overshoot, not a constant +3.33 births/year.}

In the long term, catastrophe states repeat at ever-increasing intervals, each reduces the breeding population by 90 percent, and the population finally hits a steady state at roughly one billion inhabitants.  
\textcolor{yellow}{Describing a repeating overshoot‐and‐crash cycle is good.}  
\textcolor{green}{To improve, set up a piecewise model: exponential $N(t)$ until $N=10^{10}$, then instantaneous divide by 10, then repeat.  You can derive an average population over the cycle by integrating.}

---

Summary of main issues:  
1. The student treated the birth rate as constant, leading to linear growth (real world should be exponential doubling).  
2. All time estimates for reaching thresholds are off by many orders of magnitude because of (1).  
3. The post-catastrophe regrowth was again modelled linearly instead of exponentially.  
4. Good that the student recognized the overshoot/crash behavior qualitatively and used the law correctly.
